          #RedundancyInDefinition.num_remark
            %h6#remark Remark
            %p
              This classical definition is redundant; TR4 and one direction of TR3 follow from the remaining axioms. See (
              %a{:href => "#May"}> May
              ).
            %p
              The octahedral axiom has many equivalent formulations, a concise account is given by
              = succeed "." do
                %a{:href => "#Hubery"} Hubery
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+pushouts"} homotopy pushouts
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark_2 Remark
            %p
              In the context of triangulated categories the translation functor
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                    %mo :
                    %mi C
                    %mo →
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T : C \to C
              is often called the
              %strong suspension functor
              and denoted
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo :
                    %mi X
                    %mo ↦
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (-)[1] : X \mapsto X[1]
              (in an algebraic context) or
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
              or
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
              (in a topological context). However unlike “translation functor”, suspension functor is also the term used when the invertibility is not assumed, cf.
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/suspended+category"} suspended category
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark_3 Remark
            %p
              If
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi f
                    %mo ,
                    %mi g
                    %mo ,
                    %mi h
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (f,g,h)
              is a
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/distinguished+triangle"} distinguished triangle
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi f
                    %mo ,
                    %mi g
                    %mo ,
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mi h
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (f,g,-h)
              is not generally distinguished, although it is “exact” (induces
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/long+exact+sequences"} long exact sequences
              in
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homology"} homology
              and
              = succeed ")." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohomology"} cohomology
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi f
                    %mo ,
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mi g
                    %mo ,
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mi h
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (f,-g,-h)
              %em is
              always distinguished, since it is isomorphic to
              = succeed ":" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi f
                      %mo ,
                      %mi g
                      %mo ,
                      %mi h
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (f,g,h)
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi X
                          %mtd
                            %mover
                              %mo →
                              %mi f
